T-990-Detail [5e1aa557b11e] <<LAND231>> This ticket's fix shape (record the full path in both rows from one place, keep the six detail keys, never the query string) and its four acceptance lines, read against the independent review and the builder's record. (1) The change: backend/services/accessAudit.js lines 45 to 52 hold the helper (the function at line 49 returns req.path when req.baseUrl is empty and otherwise the text of req.originalUrl before the first question mark, or the joined base and path when there is no original URL); denialDetails uses it for the refused-request row (line 59) and backend/server.js uses it for the state-change row (line 556, with the import at line 462); the two rows keep their six keys. A request answered below no router keeps req.path. (2) What the review measured, with sign-in enforced: the six keys were kept in every row of its probe (20 rows at the head, 20 on main); below no router the row keeps req.path (the rows for /api/admin/incidents and /api/settings/costs were equal on main and at the head); the query string is absent from every row whose target carried one after a question mark; an encoded question mark stays path text at both main and the head (not new); a 6000-character id records 6020 characters at the head against 6007 on main (the audit field guard bounds fields, not the audit path, before and after: pre-existing); an upper-case path records the case sent; a double slash answers 404 with the full path at both. The two shapes that are new, finding F1 Minor (introduced by this change): (a) an absolute-form target for a feedback item write wrote the refused-request row and the state-change row with the whole target, scheme and host included, as the path, where main recorded the relative id path, so a signed-in requester can put a host-looking string of its choice into the path field of an audit row; (b) a raw # in the target (/api/feedback/zzfrag#rd990probe=q) was recorded with the text after the hash, where main recorded /zzfrag, because the cut splits on a question mark only. Neither loses the true route, which is still in the row, and browsers never send either form. Both are reachable only where the helper takes its second branch, that is when a mounted router answers (the first branch returns req.path, line 50). The review's recommendation is to record only the pathname and to add two cells; the cells of the new test file (17, written for the query string) hold 0 lines that name an absolute-form target or a hash. (3) The inventory, the full verify (6086 of 6086 tests across 384 suites), the local code scan and the hosted code scan check are as in the comment on RD-793 (one pull request): the hosted check read success with No new alerts in code changed by this pull request, and the local scan read none new. (4) The cells and the proofs. The new test file holds 17 cells (U1 to U5 for the helper and for the six keys, H1 to H4 for the refused-request row in each of the three places and the second feedback write, M1 to M3 for the state-change row, B1 to B3 for rows below no router and with no session, and two source cells C1 and C2), green twice in the review; four plants on this file, each reverted by blob, turned the predicted 11, 4, 6 and 1 cells red, as the builder's table says. (5) Findings: F1 Minor (above); F4 Minor (process, no effect on the product); F5 Observation. The audit entry of the sign-in provisioning router (backend/routes/entraProvisioning.js line 167, path taken from req.path inside the router mounted at backend/server.js line 22361) is not covered by this change and was not measured by the review; read at the merge commit, not run, it has the same shape; a follow-up is proposed. (6) Not claimed, and left open: the two follow-ups above, the unbounded path length (RD-1006), and code scanning alerts (none claimed closed or changed). Not measured: a hosted instance, the production setting, a browser, and the demo (the Deploy demo run of this merge read skipped). [LAND-231-990-19NOV]
